Here's the CPA movement for the weekly report. All figures below are platform-reported media metrics (ad platforms via `compare_platform_windows`), anchor date 2026-10-30, windows as labeled by the tool (`last_week` vs `prior_week`).

## Biggest CPA change: Reddit Ads, −11.7%

| Platform | CPA prior week | CPA last week | Change | Driver |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **reddit_ads** | $125.47 | $110.75 | **−11.7%** (−$14.72) | Conversions 6 → 7 (+16.7%) on nearly flat spend ($752.84 → $775.25, +3.0%) |
| meta_ads | $197.99 | $190.00 | −4.0% (−$7.99) | Mechanical: spend cut $3,761.84 → $3,039.99 (−19.2%) outpaced conversion decline 19 → 16 (−15.8%) |
| google_ads | $192.05 | $191.18 | −0.5% (flat) | Spend up $5,377.43 → $8,029.35 (+49.3%), but conversions scaled 28 → 42 (+50.0%), so CPA held |

## What likely drove it

- **Reddit (the biggest mover):** CPA fell because conversions grew (+1 conversion) while spend barely moved. The caveat matters more here than the headline: on a base of 6→7 conversions, a single conversion swings CPA by roughly ±$16, so the −11.7% is directionally positive but statistically noisy.
- **Meta:** The improvement is real in CPA terms but it's a spending artifact — efficiency didn't improve, spend was reduced faster than conversions fell.
- **Google:** A large spend ramp (+49.3%) with conversions scaling proportionally — the account absorbed the added budget at flat efficiency, which is a favorable signal, not a CPA story.

## Caveats for the report

- **Conversions are platform-reported** (per the source-of-truth wiki). Conversion definitions differ per platform, so the three CPA figures are **not apples-to-apples** across platforms — compare each platform to its own prior week only.
- Platform conversions ≠ qualified leads; any lead-quality read would need the warehouse, which I have not joined here.
- Small conversion volumes (Reddit n=7, Meta n=16 last week) make percentage changes noisy — flag rather than over-read.
- Reddit's CPA level (~$111) sits well below Google/Meta (~$190), which may reflect a lighter conversion definition rather than true efficiency.

